= Introduction

== Higher Education in the United Kingdom
- *Durham University* is one of the oldest and most prestigious universities in the UK.
#pause
- It is located in the north-east of England, in the city of Durham.
#pause
- Classes are held not in ordinary academic buildings, but within historical and cultural monuments.
#pause
- From the very first day, studies are closely connected with real scientific research.

= History of the Foundation

== Foundation and Purpose
- *Founded:* 1832, on the initiative of Bishop William van Mildert of Durham.
#pause
- *Status:* The third oldest university in England after Oxford and Cambridge.
#pause
- *Royal Charter:* Signed by King William IV in 1837.
#pause
- *First college:* Durham Castle, built in the 11th century, was given to the university for student housing and teaching.

== Heritage Today
- Part of the university buildings belongs to the *Durham Castle and Cathedral complex*.
#pause
- The complex is protected by *UNESCO* as a World Heritage Site.
#pause
- Students therefore live and study inside centuries-old walls.

= Infrastructure, Education, and Development

== Campus Overview & Structure
- *Student Body:* About 21,730 students and more than 4,500 staff.
#pause
- *Programs:* Over 300 bachelor's and master's programs in 4 faculties, divided into 27 academic departments and schools.
#pause
- *Collegiate system:* Besides their faculty, each student belongs to one of *17 colleges* where they live, socialize, play sports and work.

== Rankings & Quality of Education
- *UK rankings (2026):* 3rd in The Times and 5th in The Guardian.
#pause
- *World ranking:* 94th in the QS ranking.
#pause
- *Research-led teaching:* Students are taught by scientists who themselves move world science forward.
#pause
- *Future-oriented skills:* Programs develop skills that will be in demand in the future.
